Brief 80: every flip-flop block of the pinned BRV32P core that has an asynchronous reset (37 blocks:
`always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)`) becomes a synchronous one (`always @(posedge clk)`); the `if (!rst_n)` bodies
are unchanged, so the reset is still active low and now acts only at a clock edge. Applied first, to the pinned copy in
build/ (run_bench.py --sync-reset). Why: brief 70 measured that an asynchronous reset blocks DSP and block-RAM register
absorption and cannot fold into the D LUT (UG949 recommends synchronous resets). Reset cycles and the benches are
unchanged (tests check this).

